							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							As an update to this unfortunate bit of drama...<br /><br />I logged on this afternoon to see Einalem online. I asked him if he had decided to return my account to me to which he responded that he had spoken to his friend (imaginary?) and talked him into giving me the account back. Supposedly his friend (cough) also admitted to selling this same account to another person for 60kpp and then stealing it back.<br /><br />Subsequently, he gave me the login info to the account (supposedly because he wanted to do the right thing - thanks to those who bombarded him with tells). I am now in the process of deleting the nodrop gear from the account and sacrificing the cleric. After all, due to the account having been so blatantly compromised for whatever reason and by whomever, it is now useless to me and the mode of operation switches more to loss management as opposed to asset preservation.<br /><br />All of my dealings with this piece of scum were via the telephone. If he has screwed anyone else contact me in game and I will hook ya up with his name and number. Contemplated posting it here but that is in bad taste and I am trying to handle this as maturely as I can given how angry I am.<br /><br />Incidentally, the guild leader of Apotheosis just sent me a tell in game and apparantly decided to remove Einalem from the guild over this issue (/cheer Qadain and apoth).<br /><br />End result... Einalem = scammer and a prick. Apotheosis = pretty cool based upon how they handled this.<br /><br />-Az<br /><br />Oh! Forgot to mention that he had removed the belt of thunderous auras from Breneaur prior to giving me the account back. Way to do the right thing lol.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
